"comparison": "Both Intel and Micron address the capital intensity and investment strategy required for leading-edge manufacturing, but differ in their market focus and specific capacity challenges. Intel characterizes the industry-wide trend where high capital intensity has forced most manufacturers to stop pursuing Moore's Law due to insufficient scale, asserting Intel is one of only three pursuing 2nm and aiming to amortize these investments over many years. Micron similarly discusses its need to add new DRAM wafer capacity for the second half of the decade, funded by the CHIPS Act, with specific timelines for construction and output. While both disclose large-scale capital investments in leading-edge facilities, Intel focuses on the strategic difficulty of amortizing costs in a general semiconductor foundry context, whereas Micron details specific fab construction plans for memory chips.",
