"relationship": "related_topic",
"comparison": "Both Intel and Micron disclose operational adjustments related to capital efficiency and manufacturing node transitions, though their immediate market conditions differ. Intel describes a strategic approach where foundries maximize volume and pricing on leading-edge designs in early years, shifting to manufacturing cost and ease of design as technologies mature. Micron provides a concrete example of this dynamic in reverse: it proactively lowered structural capacity by reducing capital expenditures and wafer starts, and redeployed equipment from older nodes to leading-edge nodes to improve capital efficiency, which resulted in a structural reduction in wafer capacity. While Intel discusses the ideal amortization strategy for new nodes, Micron illustrates the immediate financial and capacity impacts of transitioning to and managing those leading-edge nodes amidst challenging market conditions.",
